## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of user-space vs kernel-space concept interplay, synchronization primitives, and designing a lazy I/O cache with proper wakeups.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block state machine (EMPTY, PENDING, LOADED, ERROR)
- Fast path vs slow path with contention handling
- Wait queues (futex/condvar) and wakeup semantics
- Avoiding thundering herd with CAS and double-checked locking
- Error propagation from I/O to callers

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  void *data;
  atomic_int state; // 0=EMPTY,1=PENDING,2=LOADED,3=ERROR
  int waiters;
  pthread_mutex_t mutex;
  pthread_cond_t cond;
} cache_entry;

#define EMPTY 0
#define PENDING 1
#define LOADED 2
#define ERROR 3

void* block_read(int block_id) {
  cache_entry *entry = &cache[block_id];

  // Fast path
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    return entry->data;
  }

  pthread_mutex_lock(&entry->mutex);

  // Double-check after acquiring lock
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);
    return entry->data;
  }

  // Transition to PENDING if first thread
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&entry->state, &EMPTY, PENDING)) {
    enqueue_io_request(block_id);
  }

  // Block until I/O completes
  while (atomic_load(&entry->state) == PENDING) {
    entry->waiters++;
    pthread_cond_wait(&entry->cond, &entry->mutex);
    entry->waiters--;
  }

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);

  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == ERROR) {
    return NULL; // Propagate error
  }

  return entry->data;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of disk scheduling algorithms, end-to-end flow from I/O request to service, and practical data structure choices in kernel-like environments.

## Key Concepts
- **Elevator (SCAN) vs SSTF**: SCAN provides more uniform latency by serving all requests in sweep order, while SSTF can cause starvation for distant requests.
- **Direction management**: Track current direction and handle boundary reversal at min/max sector addresses.
- **Tie-breaking**: When multiple requests target the same sector, FIFO ordering by arrival time ensures fairness.
- **Fairness considerations**: SCAN inherently favors requests at disk edges but avoids starvation. For stronger fairness guarantees, C-SCAN (circular SCAN) provides equal service time per full sweep, or deadline-based schedulers can prioritize older requests.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Req {
    int sector;
    int arrival;
    int pid;
    struct Req* next;
} Req;

Req* enqueue(Req* head, Req* r, int dir) {
    // Insert sorted by sector; if equal, preserve arrival order
    if (!head || r->sector < head->sector || 
        (r->sector == head->sector && r->arrival < head->arrival)) {
        r->next = head;
        return r;
    }
    Req* cur = head;
    while (cur->next && (cur->next->sector < r->sector ||
        (cur->next->sector == r->sector && cur->next->arrival <= r->arrival)))
        cur = cur->next;
    r->next = cur->next;
    cur->next = r;
    return head;
}

Req* next_request(Req* head, int* dir, int minSector, int maxSector) {
    if (!head) return NULL;
    // Seek in current direction
    Req* best = NULL;
    Req** prev = &head;
    Req* cur = head;
    while (cur) {
        if ((*dir > 0 && cur->sector >= 0) || (*dir < 0 && cur->sector <= 0)) {
            if (!best) { best = cur; *prev = cur->next; break; }
        }
        prev = &cur->next;
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    // If no request in current direction, reverse and try again
    if (!best) { *dir = -*dir; return next_request(head, dir, minSector, maxSector); }
    return best;
}
```

